### What does `collectingAndThen` do?

`collectingAndThen` takes an existing collector and a finisher function. It runs the collector to produce a result, then passes that result through the finisher and returns whatever the finisher returns. It is a way to add one final transformation to a collecting pipeline.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ImmutableNames {
    public static List<String> names(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
            .map(Employee::getName)
            .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
                Collectors.toList(),             // downstream: gather into a list
                Collections::unmodifiableList)); // finisher: make it read-only
    }
}
```

### How does it wrap a collector with a finishing step?

Internally a collector has a finisher stage. `collectingAndThen` replaces or extends that stage by composing your function after the downstream collector finishes. The flow is: supply container, accumulate elements, finish the downstream, then apply your finisher.

```text
  downstream.supplier()     -> new ArrayList
  downstream.accumulator()  -> add each name
  downstream.finisher()     -> the ArrayList
  your finisher             -> Collections.unmodifiableList(list)
  returned result           -> read-only List<String>
```

### How do you build an unmodifiable id-to-name map?

Use `toMap` as the downstream collector and `Collections::unmodifiableMap` as the finisher.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class ImmutableLookup {
    public static Map<Long, String> idToName(List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
            .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
                Collectors.toMap(Employee::getId, Employee::getName),
                Collections::unmodifiableMap));
    }
}
```

```text
{101=Rajesh, 102=Priya, 103=Pankaj}
```

Any attempt to modify the returned map now fails.

### Why is it used for immutability?

Streams often collect into a mutable `ArrayList` or `HashMap`. If you return that directly, any caller can change it and introduce bugs. `collectingAndThen` lets you return a read-only view in the same expression, so the mutable container never escapes.

```java
package com.bank.hr;

import java.util.List;

public class ReadOnlyDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
            new Employee(101L, "Rajesh"),
            new Employee(102L, "Priya"));
        List<String> names = ImmutableNames.names(employees);
        names.add("Intruder"); // not allowed
    }
}
```

```text
Exception in thread "main" java.lang.UnsupportedOperationException
	at java.base/java.util.Collections$UnmodifiableCollection.add(Collections.java:1067)
```

### How is this different from collecting then wrapping on a separate line?

The result is similar, but `collectingAndThen` keeps everything in one pipeline and never exposes the mutable container to the surrounding code. The two-line version creates a named mutable variable that could be mutated before wrapping.

| Approach | Mutable container exposed? | Lines | Risk |
|----------|----------------------------|-------|------|
| Collect then wrap separately | yes, as a local variable | two statements | can be mutated before wrapping |
| `collectingAndThen` | no, wrapped inline | one expression | mutable container never escapes |

```java
// Two-line version: the mutable list exists as a variable first
List<String> tmp = employees.stream().map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toList());
List<String> readOnly = Collections.unmodifiableList(tmp);

// One-expression version: no intermediate variable to misuse
List<String> readOnly2 = employees.stream().map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
        Collectors.toList(), Collections::unmodifiableList));
```

### How is `unmodifiableList` different from `List.copyOf` and `toUnmodifiableList`?

`unmodifiableList` returns a read-only view over the original list, so changes to the original still show through. `List.copyOf` and `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()` create a truly independent immutable copy.

| Technique | Independent copy? | Reflects backing changes? | Allows nulls? |
|-----------|-------------------|---------------------------|---------------|
| `Collections.unmodifiableList(list)` | no, a view | yes | yes (if backing allows) |
| `List.copyOf(list)` | yes | no | no, rejects nulls |
| `Collectors.toUnmodifiableList()` | yes | n/a | no, rejects nulls |

```java
// Truly immutable, no backing list to leak (Java 10+)
List<String> names = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
```

### What are the common mistakes?

The first mistake is keeping a reference to the backing collection, so a read-only view still changes underneath.

```java
// WRONG: the view reflects later mutations of the backing list
List<String> backing = new java.util.ArrayList<>(List.of("Rajesh"));
List<String> view = Collections.unmodifiableList(backing);
backing.add("Priya");          // mutates the backing list
System.out.println(view);      // [Rajesh, Priya] - surprise!
```

```java
// CORRECT: use collectingAndThen so no backing reference escapes,
// or use toUnmodifiableList for a true copy
List<String> view = employees.stream().map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
```

The second mistake is expecting the view to be modifiable and calling `add` or `put`, which throws `UnsupportedOperationException`.

### What are the edge cases with nulls?

`collectingAndThen` with `toUnmodifiableList` or `List.copyOf` rejects null elements. `toMap` already rejects null values. If your data can contain nulls and you want an unmodifiable result, filter them first or use `unmodifiableList` over a list that permits nulls.

```java
// Rejects null, throws NullPointerException if a name is null
List<String> names = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());

// Filter nulls before collecting if they can occur
List<String> safe = employees.stream()
    .map(Employee::getName)
    .filter(java.util.Objects::nonNull)
    .collect(Collectors.toUnmodifiableList());
```

### When should you use `collectingAndThen`?

Use it whenever a collecting pipeline needs one final transformation: wrapping for immutability, converting a collected list into an array, or converting a count into a report object. Reach for `toUnmodifiableList`/`toUnmodifiableMap` directly when you only need an immutable copy and nothing else.

```java
// Finisher that is not about immutability: list -> count summary
String summary = employees.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.collectingAndThen(
        Collectors.counting(),
        count -> "Total employees: " + count));
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **Views versus copies.** `Collections.unmodifiable*` returns a live view; `List.copyOf` and `toUnmodifiableList` return an independent copy. Prefer copies when the backing data may change.
- **Null policy.** The Java 9+ immutable factories and collectors reject null; the older `unmodifiable*` wrappers do not.
- **Composability.** `collectingAndThen` composes with any downstream collector, including `groupingBy`, letting you make the grouped lists immutable: `groupingBy(dept, collectingAndThen(toList(), List::copyOf))`.
- **Performance.** The finisher runs once at the end, so it adds negligible cost compared to the collection itself.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What are the two arguments to `collectingAndThen`?" Answer: a downstream collector and a finisher function.
- "Why use it instead of wrapping on a separate line?" Answer: it keeps the mutable container from escaping and keeps the pipeline to one expression.
- "Difference between `unmodifiableList` and `toUnmodifiableList`?" Answer: the former is a view over the original; the latter is an independent immutable copy that rejects nulls.
- "What exception do you get modifying the result?" Answer: `UnsupportedOperationException`.
